SPU lifter: functions with a loop keep their registers in locals (a hot job loop 9-17% faster per call).

The lifter writes every register access as ctx->gpr[n]. A local-store store goes through ctx->ls, and with
-fno-strict-aliasing it may alias the context, so after each one the compiler re-reads every register from ctx and
every result is stored back at once: a lifted loop runs through memory. In the Resistance 3 Haven fight the hottest
lifted SPU loop (igjob_00E29200 at 0x2E8) loaded registers from ctx 86 times in its body.

tools/spu_regcache.py runs on the lifter's output (emit_source; PS3RECOMP_SPU_REGCACHE=0 emits the old form, and so
does a --trace lift, whose spu_trace_rt prints ctx->gpr). For a function with a goto back to a label above it, the
registers it names live in locals: loaded on entry, stored back before any statement that hands ctx to code that may
read them (a lifted call, the drain, spu_indirect_branch, stop/halt, channel ops, SPU_DIRECT/SPU_RET, return),
re-read at the end of a line that may run other SPU code. The lifter emits one balanced statement per body line, and
such a call sits in plain blocks with no register access or goto after it on its line; a function with another shape
is left as it is (none in Resistance 3). Functions without a loop are left alone: caching every function grew the
lifted SPU code by 61% and a straight chunk with five exits ran no faster; loops only: +7.5% (1651 of 16119
functions). The LS diagnostics read ctx->gpr themselves (SPU_LS_WATCH r0, SPU_LS_LOWREAD r1-r4) and see the values
of the last store-back.
Idea from the Resistance 2 port's spu_regcache.py / spu_writeback.py (MIT,
github.com/miroslavdanutalexandru-alt/Resistance2Port); this is a separate implementation.

Test: tools/test_spu_lifter_regcache.py lifts a loop (LS store and load, a call to a subroutine that changes the
caller's register, a stop) with and without the cache; both runs must give the expected registers and LS (the
store-back before a call placed after it fails it), the loop body must not name ctx->gpr, a loopless function and a
trace lift are left alone. Unit cases: a store-back is dropped right after another and kept after a write or a label,
none goes between a call and its reload, a comment is not code, and a call the reload may not follow (conditional,
register access or goto after it) leaves the function alone.
Micro-benchmark (registers and LS of three entries of each function dumped from the Haven fight with lldb, the
function run 3000-5000 times from each, both versions from the same lift): igjob_00E29200 0x2E8 458-542 ->
417-458 ns per call (9-17%), jobmod_8f987d5458b2 0x4300 (no loop) unchanged; the registers and LS after the call
are bit-identical in all six cases. In-game fps could not be compared: the fight is not SPU-bound since gcm-rsx/0024
and run-to-run noise is larger than lifted SPU code's share.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
